p(49,3,"The buyers and sellers, too, many of them, looked not much better off",
 "At the sale, the buyers and sellers are poor too. Some look hard from hard times, others are kind. A man with a boy comes to the horse and looks at him with pity.",
 "The buyers and sellers, many of them, did not look much better off than the poor animals they were bargaining about. There were poor old men trying to get a horse or a pony for a few pounds, to pull some little wood or coal cart. There were poor men trying to sell a worn-out horse for two or three pounds, rather than have the greater loss of killing him. Some of them looked as if poverty and hard times had made them hard all over. But there were others for whom I would gladly have used the last of my strength. They were poor and shabby, but kind and human, with voices that I could trust. One weak old man liked me very much, and I liked him, but I was not strong enough. It was an anxious time! Coming from the better part of the fair, I noticed a man who looked like a gentleman farmer, with a young boy by his side. He had a broad back and round shoulders and a kind, red face, and he wore a hat with a wide edge. When he came up to me and my companions, he stood still and gave a sad look round at us. I saw his eye rest on me. I still had a good mane and tail, which did something for how I looked. I raised my ears and looked at him.",
 ["bargaining","poverty","shabby","tottering","pitiful","pricked"])
s(49,"I’ll be bound he’d be worth twenty pounds next spring",
 "I am sure he will be worth twenty pounds next spring.",
 "'I’ll be bound' means 'I am sure'; 'he’d be worth' is 'he would be worth', and it is a guess about next spring.")
